    "@id": "https://api.nuget.org/v3/catalog0/data/2026.09.27.17.54.06/invarix.gate.extensions.ai.1.0.0-rc.2.json#deprecation",
    "alternatePackage": {
      "@id": "https://api.nuget.org/v3/catalog0/data/2026.09.27.17.54.06/invarix.gate.extensions.ai.1.0.0-rc.2.json#deprecation/alternatePackage",
      "id": "Invarix.Gate.Extensions.AI",
      "range": "[1.0.0-rc.3, )"
    },
    "message": "Superseded by 1.0.0-rc.3 of the Invarix.Gate packages. rc.3 fixes approval requests that could carry credentials unmasked (advisory INVARIX-2026-001, https://invarix.dk/security), an Agent Framework defect that, in enforce mode and with no GateRunScope, terminated every run once the agent's total tool calls passed the per-run ceiling, and the evidence recorded for approvals nobody answered. The rc.2 README's example policy ran 3 of the 11 detectors; re-check any policy copied from it.",
    "reasons": [
      "Other",
      "CriticalBugs"
    ]
  },
  "description": "Microsoft.Extensions.AI adapter for Invarix.Gate, the deterministic action firewall for AI agents on .NET. One UseInvarixGate() call on a ChatClientBuilder puts every tool call through the policy engine before it executes and taps the chat boundary for token usage, so per-run and per-agent ceilings see spend as it happens rather than after the run. MCP client tools are covered through the same interception point, because they are AIFunctions. Observe mode is the default and blocks nothing. Gate covers the tools invoked through the wrapped pipeline; the run report publishes what it could not see.",
  "iconFile": "nuget-icon-128.png",
  "id": "Invarix.Gate.Extensions.AI",
  "isPrerelease": true,
